{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>Thoughts</b> — of a diligent man." "But only to a surplus — that he has advantage in his engagement in trade, whereas the wicked mentioned have but little profit." "And every hasty one — like the expression 'hasty with feet' (Proverbs 6:18), that he runs to evil, because poverty overtakes him, so he is hasty and rushes; but only to a small surplus, that of the diligent."], :book :proverbs, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {2 "The final clause 'אך למעט מותר החרוץ' is textually difficult; read hyper-literally as 'but only a little is the surplus of the diligent,' though the syntax is strained and may reflect corruption."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Proverbs -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/mishley/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:23:56.180899391Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}